# Environment variables, via the httpfly.env.json sitting next to this
# file. Run :HttpEnv (or <leader>he) first and pick "local" or "local-ip"
# -- both point at the same local httpbin container, just via hostname vs
# IP, to demonstrate actually switching between named environments. Then
# :HttpSend / :HttpSendAll as usual. Run :HttpEnvVars (<leader>hv) any time
# to see exactly what {{base_url}} and {{client_name}} currently resolve
# to for the selected environment.
#
# {{base_url}} comes from whichever environment you picked; {{client_name}}
# comes from "shared", so it's the same value regardless of which
# environment is selected. Precedence, highest to lowest: a request's own
# local variable > client.global (persisted, see 2_scripting.http) >
# the selected environment (shared, overridden by its own entry) > this
# file's own global (prelude) variable.
#
# httpfly.env.json is a single file -- unlike some other HTTP-file tools,
# there's no separate "private" file split out for secrets. If you need to
# keep values out of version control, .gitignore the whole file (or a
# project-specific copy of it) rather than relying on a built-in split.
#
# httpfly looks for httpfly.env.json in its own process's cwd only, no
# upward directory search -- httpfly.nvim always runs it with that cwd set
# to this .http file's own directory, so the file has to sit right here,
# next to the .http files that use it.
#
# Run `make httpbin-up` from the repo root first (requires Docker).

###
# @name Get
GET {{base_url}}/get?client={{client_name}} HTTP/1.1
